WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.528
Emperor Yang of Sui ordered the building of a Grand Canal running from north to south, to bind China's south and north closely together.

00:00:09.878 --> 00:00:19.286
Tens of thousands of workers dug earth and carried stones together, laboring under the blazing sun — a truly spectacular scene.

00:00:19.636 --> 00:00:22.804
Many workers were drenched in sweat from exhaustion.

00:00:23.154 --> 00:00:30.546
After years of effort, the canal was finally completed, its waters flowing from the north all the way to the south.

00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:34.184
From then on, travel between north and south became far easier.

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:43.366
Great boats loaded with grain and goods came and went one after another, while merchants on the banks busily traded — it was lively beyond measure.

00:00:43.716 --> 00:00:47.580
From then on, grain from the south could be shipped to the north.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:56.234
More than a thousand years have passed, and the Grand Canal still flows quietly on, while the towns along its banks grow ever more prosperous.

00:00:56.584 --> 00:01:00.232
It is one of the greatest engineering works of ancient China.

